<BaseLayout title="Methodology — How We Produce Neutral, Source-Backed Analysis" description="Our editorial methodology: neutrality, sourcing standards, and verification.">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="muted">How we produce neutral, source-backed policy and trade analysis.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:20px;">1. Neutrality</h2>
  <p>We analyze policy and trade developments without political bias. We do not evaluate foreign leaders subjectively, and we do not produce inflammatory or nationally divisive content.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:20px;">2. Sourcing standards</h2>
  <p>We rely only on primary and official sources: government publications, central banks, trade ministries, and official regional bodies (e.g., ASEAN). We cross-verify each event from at least two independent sources before writing. Conflicting sources cause the topic to be dropped, not forced.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:20px;">3. Verification & the five-part data discipline</h2>
  <p>Every figure carries: value + unit + time + named source + fetch date. Unverifiable figures are marked "pending verification" and such articles are not published.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:20px;">4. Corrections</h2>
  <p>When underlying facts change materially, we publish a public correction in the article's revision history rather than silently editing dates.</p>

  <h2 style="font-size:20px;">5. Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>All content is policy and trade analysis for informational purposes only and does not constitute investment or legal advice.</p>
</BaseLayout>
